WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.868
[music playing]

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.105
[clock ticking]

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:07.407
NARRATOR: Not everything goes
the way you want it to go.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.110
You don't get to choose
how life unfolds.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.478
You just get to live it.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:25.892
Looks like I've got
good moving water,

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.861
but it looks like it's
out there quite a ways.

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.601
Right now here in Kavik, this
is the changing of the seasons.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:38.004
Winter is letting go and
the springtime is coming in.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:39.939
My list of chores is long.

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:42.542
But the most important
one that opens up first

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.577
is get running water to camp.

00:00:44.644 --> 00:00:47.480
How do I get water?

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:48.615
I have hose.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.884
I have to run it all the
way down to the river,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.553
put it in the river,
pump it all the way up,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.055
filter it, treat it, and
then send it around camp

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:57.023
where I want it.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.462
That's stagnant right they're.

00:01:02.529 --> 00:01:04.297
Not interested in that.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:08.034
Why is it important to
have rapidly moving water?

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.770
In a stagnant pond, what
do you get if you're down

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.939
south maybe in the lower world?

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:17.477
Tadpoles, slime-- you're
going to get sick.

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:21.815
So stagnant water--
big fat no, no.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:26.286
Moving water keeps it
clean, keeps it oxygenated.

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:29.122
That's what you want.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:32.859
I've got nice water action
coming down this way.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.360
It's moving.

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:38.765
I may be able to just
get it right here.

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:41.568
The melting ice doesn't
have a lot of issues.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.838
So what I think I want to
do is get the water line,

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.773
set it out so it
can finish thawing,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:47.907
and then go from there.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:56.850
You can see my pipe is encased
in a little bit of ice.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:02:00.253
So my favorite piece of
equipment is a sledge.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.824
When you run into a problem, you
can just kind of sledge it out.

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:11.197
Alaska is known
for its resources--

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:16.269
oil, gold-- but the one as
residents we desire the most

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.637
is that liquid water.

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:20.707
That's what we call the gold.

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:24.611
I've had a good nine, 10 months
with no running water in camp.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:27.647
A nice hot shower
is not a bad thing.

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:30.850
And especially this last year
with COVID, the importance

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:34.687
of keeping everything
hygienically clean

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:36.990
can't be overstressed.

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:40.493
Who said Mike
doesn't make right?

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.629
Wasn't somebody
with a sledgehammer.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:43.763
I guarantee you that.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:53.606
Not bad for a fat old
chick on the tundra.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.008
That's the pipe.

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:56.576
And it can lay out here.

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:59.312
There's going to be
frozen bits inside.

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:03.917
Even in a cloudy state, that
sun will work on that, melt it,

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:08.955
so when I need to use it, it's
free of that and it can flow.

00:03:09.022 --> 00:03:12.492
I'll let this be, let
nature start working on it,

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:16.930
go inside, and warm up.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.732
[music playing]

